). Yield of Trial A at the plot found in part (a) (kg).","points":0.5,"difficulty":"hard","topic":"Quadratic sequences","config":{"source":"myp5-sequences-series-e-assessment","question_number":33,"part":"b1","chapter":"Quadratic sequences","criterion":"D","printed_level":"Level 6","printed_question_marks":5,"marks_printed_in_pdf":"question total only","numeric_answer":254.0,"tolerance":0.0},"options":[]},{"position":70,"type":"numeric","prompt":"Q33 (b). Yield of Trial B at the plot found in part (a) (kg).","points":0.5,"difficulty":"hard","topic":"Quadratic sequences","config":{"source":"myp5-sequences-series-e-assessment","question_number":33,"part":"b2","chapter":"Quadratic sequences","criterion":"D","printed_level":"Level 6","printed_question_marks":5,"marks_printed_in_pdf":"question total only","numeric_answer":241.0,"tolerance":0.0},"options":[]},{"position":71,"type":"open_ended","prompt":"Q33 (c). Interpret the crossing point and explain why checking only the equality plot is not enough.","points":2,"difficulty":"hard","topic":"Quadratic sequences","config":{"source":"myp5-sequences-series-e-assessment","question_number":33,"part":"c","chapter":"Quadratic sequences","criterion":"D","printed_level":"Level 6","printed_question_marks":5,"marks_printed_in_pdf":"question total only","marking_criteria":"2 marks. 1: the two yields are equal at plot 12, and Trial A is greater from plot 13 onward. 1: checking only the equal point does not show which trial is greater after it; the inequality n^2 - 12n > 0 must be checked."},"options":[]},{"position":72,"type":"numeric","prompt":"Q34 (a). A phone's value at the start of each year is 8,100, 5,400, 3,600, 2,400 XOF (a geometric sequence). Find the common ratio r.","points":0.5,"difficulty":"easy","topic":"Geometric sequences","config":{"source":"myp5-sequences-series-e-assessment","question_number":34,"part":"a","chapter":"Geometric sequences","criterion":"A","printed_level":"Level 1","printed_question_marks":1,"marks_printed_in_pdf":"question total only","numeric_answer":0.666667,"tolerance":0.001},"options":[]},{"position":73,"type":"numeric","prompt":"Q34 (b). A phone's value at the start of each year is 8,100, 5,400, 3,600, 2,400 XOF (a geometric sequence). Find the next value (XOF).","points":0.5,"difficulty":"easy","topic":"Geometric sequences","config":{"source":"myp5-sequences-series-e-assessment","question_number":34,"part":"b","chapter":"Geometric sequences","criterion":"A","printed_level":"Level 1","printed_question_marks":1,"marks_printed_in_pdf":"question total only","numeric_answer":1600.0,"tolerance":0.0},"options":[]},{"position":74,"type":"open_ended","prompt":"Q35 (a). A bacteria culture has 1,200 bacteria at hour 1 and the count increases by 8% each hour. Write a formula for u_n.","points":0.5,"difficulty":"easy","topic":"Geometric sequences","config":{"source":"myp5-sequences-series-e-assessment","question_number":35,"part":"a","chapter":"Geometric sequences","criterion":"D","printed_level":"Level 2","printed_question_marks":1,"marks_printed_in_pdf":"question total only","marking_criteria":"0.5 mark. u_n = 1200 × (1.08)^(n-1), or an equivalent form."},"options":[]},{"position":75,"type":"numeric","prompt":"Q35 (b). A bacteria culture has 1,200 bacteria at hour 1 and increases by 8% each hour. Find the count at hour 5, rounded to the nearest whole bacterium.","points":0.5,"difficulty":"easy","topic":"Geometric sequences","config":{"source":"myp5-sequences-series-e-assessment","question_number":35,"part":"b","chapter":"Geometric sequences","criterion":"D","printed_level":"Level 2","printed_question_marks":1,"marks_printed_in_pdf":"question total only","numeric_answer":1633.0,"tolerance":0.0},"options":[]},{"position":76,"type":"matching","prompt":"Q36. Match each geometric sequence to its explicit rule. Use each choice once.","points":2,"difficulty":"medium","topic":"Geometric sequences","config":{"source":"myp5-sequences-series-e-assessment","question_number":36,"part":"-","chapter":"Geometric sequences","criterion":"A","printed_level":"Level 3","printed_question_marks":2,"marks_printed_in_pdf":"question total only","match_mode":"match","pairs":[{"left":"$a_1 = 5,\\; r = 2$","right":"$u_n = 5 \\times 2^{n-1}$"},{"left":"$a_1 = 96,\\; r = \\tfrac{1}{2}$","right":"$u_n = 96 \\times (\\tfrac{1}{2})^{n-1}$"},{"left":"$a_1 = 3,\\; r = -2$","right":"$u_n = 3 \\times (-2)^{n-1}$"},{"left":"$a_1 = 7,\\; r = 3$","right":"$u_n = 7 \\times 3^{n-1}$"}]},"options":[]},{"position":77,"type":"open_ended","prompt":"Q37. Salary Plan A starts at 30,000 XOF and rises by 2,500 XOF each year. Plan B starts at 28,000 XOF and rises by 10% each year. Compare the year 6 values. Explain which plan grows by a constant amount and which grows by a constant factor.","points":2,"difficulty":"medium","topic":"Geometric sequences","config":{"source":"myp5-sequences-series-e-assessment","question_number":37,"part":"-","chapter":"Geometric sequences","criterion":"C","printed_level":"Level 4","printed_question_marks":2,"marks_printed_in_pdf":"question total only","marking_criteria":"2 marks. 1: year 6 values: Plan A = 42,500 XOF, Plan B = 28,000 × 1.1^5 ≈ 45,094 XOF, so Plan B is greater. 1: Plan A grows by a constant amount (arithmetic), Plan B by a constant factor (geometric)."},"options":[]},{"position":78,"type":"open_ended","prompt":"Q38 (a). A student writes r = 6 - 3 = 3 and u_n = 3 × 3^(n-1) for 3, 6, 12, 24, ... . Identify the confusion.","points":1,"difficulty":"medium","topic":"Geometric sequences","config":{"source":"myp5-sequences-series-e-assessment","question_number":38,"part":"a","chapter":"Geometric sequences","criterion":"C","printed_level":"Level 4","printed_question_marks":2,"marks_printed_in_pdf":"question total only","marking_criteria":"1 mark. The student subtracted terms (6 - 3) to find a ratio. The ratio is found by dividing: r = 6 ÷ 3 = 2."},"options":[]},{"position":79,"type":"numeric","prompt":"Q38 (b). For 3, 6, 12, 24, ... , find the correct common ratio r.","points":0.5,"difficulty":"medium","topic":"Geometric sequences","config":{"source":"myp5-sequences-series-e-assessment","question_number":38,"part":"b","chapter":"Geometric sequences","criterion":"C","printed_level":"Level 4","printed_question_marks":2,"marks_printed_in_pdf":"question total only","numeric_answer":2.0,"tolerance":0.0},"options":[]},{"position":80,"type":"open_ended","prompt":"Q38 (c). For 3, 6, 12, 24, ... , write the correct explicit rule.","points":0.5,"difficulty":"medium","topic":"Geometric sequences","config":{"source":"myp5-sequences-series-e-assessment","question_number":38,"part":"c","chapter":"Geometric sequences","criterion":"C","printed_level":"Level 4","printed_question_marks":2,"marks_printed_in_pdf":"question total only","marking_criteria":"0.5 mark. u_n = 3 × 2^(n-1), or an equivalent form."},"options":[]},{"position":81,"type":"matching","prompt":"Q39. Sort the real-life descriptions into arithmetic, geometric and neither. Use every tile once.","points":2,"difficulty":"medium","topic":"Geometric sequences","config":{"source":"myp5-sequences-series-e-assessment","question_number":39,"part":"-","chapter":"Geometric sequences","criterion":"B","printed_level":"Level 4","printed_question_marks":2,"marks_printed_in_pdf":"question total only","match_mode":"sort","pairs":[{"left":"A fare rises by 50 XOF each kilometre.","right":"Arithmetic"},{"left":"A tree's height multiplies by 1.1 each year.","right":"Geometric"},{"left":"A battery keeps 92% of its charge each hour.","right":"Geometric"},{"left":"A stall sells 4 more bags each day.","right":"Arithmetic"},{"left":"Daily sales are 20, 21, 23, 26 bags.","right":"Neither"},{"left":"Crop yields are 10, 12, 15, 19 tonnes.","right":"Neither"}]},"options":[]},{"position":82,"type":"numeric","prompt":"Q40 (a). A solar pump is worth 480,000 XOF at the start of year 1, and 82% of the previous year's value at the start of each later year. Find the first year in which the value is below 200,000 XOF.","points":1,"difficulty":"hard","topic":"Geometric sequences","config":{"source":"myp5-sequences-series-e-assessment","question_number":40,"part":"a","chapter":"Geometric sequences","criterion":"D","printed_level":"Level 5","printed_question_marks":2,"marks_printed_in_pdf":"question total only","numeric_answer":6.0,"tolerance":0.0},"options":[]},{"position":83,"type":"numeric","prompt":"Q40 (b). A solar pump is worth 480,000 XOF at the start of year 1, and 82% of the previous year's value at the start of each later year. Find the value in that year, to the nearest 100 XOF.","points":1,"difficulty":"hard","topic":"Geometric sequences","config":{"source":"myp5-sequences-series-e-assessment","question_number":40,"part":"b","chapter":"Geometric sequences","criterion":"D","printed_level":"Level 5","printed_question_marks":2,"marks_printed_in_pdf":"question total only","numeric_answer":178000.0,"tolerance":0.0},"options":[]},{"position":84,"type":"open_ended","prompt":"Q41. A student says 2, -6, 18, -54, ... is not geometric because the differences change. Justify whether the sequence is geometric. Explain the role of the negative common ratio.","points":2,"difficulty":"hard","topic":"Geometric sequences","config":{"source":"myp5-sequences-series-e-assessment","question_number":41,"part":"-","chapter":"Geometric sequences","criterion":"C","printed_level":"Level 5","printed_question_marks":2,"marks_printed_in_pdf":"question total only","marking_criteria":"2 marks. 1: the sequence is geometric because the ratio u_(n+1)/u_n = -3 is constant, even though the differences change. 1: the negative ratio makes the terms alternate in sign."},"options":[]},{"position":85,"type":"open_ended","prompt":"Q42 (a). A clinic records 6,400 mosquitoes in a treated area at week 1. Each later week the count is 75% of the previous week's count. Write a formula for u_n.","points":1,"difficulty":"hard","topic":"Geometric sequences","config":{"source":"myp5-sequences-series-e-assessment","question_number":42,"part":"a","chapter":"Geometric sequences","criterion":"D","printed_level":"Level 6","printed_question_marks":6,"marks_printed_in_pdf":"question total only","marking_criteria":"1 mark. u_n = 6400 × (0.75)^(n-1), or an equivalent form."},"options":[]},{"position":86,"type":"numeric","prompt":"Q42 (b). Mosquito counts: u_1 = 6,400 and each later week is 75% of the previous week. Find the first week in which the count is below 1,000.","points":2,"difficulty":"hard","topic":"Geometric sequences","config":{"source":"myp5-sequences-series-e-assessment","question_number":42,"part":"b","chapter":"Geometric sequences","criterion":"D","printed_level":"Level 6","printed_question_marks":6,"marks_printed_in_pdf":"question total only","numeric_answer":8.0,"tolerance":0.0},"options":[]},{"position":87,"type":"numeric","prompt":"Q42 (c). Mosquito counts: u_1 = 6,400 and each later week is 75% of the previous week. Give the count in the week found in part (b), rounded to the nearest whole mosquito.","points":1,"difficulty":"hard","topic":"Geometric sequences","config":{"source":"myp5-sequences-series-e-assessment","question_number":42,"part":"c","chapter":"Geometric sequences","criterion":"D","printed_level":"Level 6","printed_question_marks":6,"marks_printed_in_pdf":"question total only","numeric_answer":854.0,"tolerance":0.0},"options":[]},{"position":88,"type":"open_ended","prompt":"Q42 (d). Interpret whether this model predicts exactly zero mosquitoes in any finite week.","points":2,"difficulty":"hard","topic":"Geometric sequences","config":{"source":"myp5-sequences-series-e-assessment","question_number":42,"part":"d","chapter":"Geometric sequences","criterion":"D","printed_level":"Level 6","printed_question_marks":6,"marks_printed_in_pdf":"question total only","marking_criteria":"2 marks. The model is geometric with ratio 0.75, so every term is positive and the count only approaches zero; it never reaches exactly zero in any finite week."},"options":[]},{"position":89,"type":"numeric","prompt":"Q43 (a). An arithmetic sequence has a_1 = 12, d = 5 and n = 10. For S_n = n/2 [2a_1 + (n-1)d], find 2a_1.","points":0.33,"difficulty":"easy","topic":"Arithmetic series","config":{"source":"myp5-sequences-series-e-assessment","question_number":43,"part":"a","chapter":"Arithmetic series","criterion":"A","printed_level":"Level 2","printed_question_marks":1,"marks_printed_in_pdf":"question total only","numeric_answer":24.0,"tolerance":0.0},"options":[]},{"position":90,"type":"numeric","prompt":"Q43 (b). An arithmetic sequence has a_1 = 12, d = 5 and n = 10. For S_n = n/2 [2a_1 + (n-1)d], find (n-1)d.","points":0.33,"difficulty":"easy","topic":"Arithmetic series","config":{"source":"myp5-sequences-series-e-assessment","question_number":43,"part":"b","chapter":"Arithmetic series","criterion":"A","printed_level":"Level 2","printed_question_marks":1,"marks_printed_in_pdf":"question total only","numeric_answer":45.0,"tolerance":0.0},"options":[]},{"position":91,"type":"numeric","prompt":"Q43 (c). An arithmetic sequence has a_1 = 12, d = 5 and n = 10. Find S_10.","points":0.34,"difficulty":"easy","topic":"Arithmetic series","config":{"source":"myp5-sequences-series-e-assessment","question_number":43,"part":"c","chapter":"Arithmetic series","criterion":"A","printed_level":"Level 2","printed_question_marks":1,"marks_printed_in_pdf":"question total only","numeric_answer":345.0,"tolerance":0.0},"options":[]},{"position":92,"type":"numeric","prompt":"Q44. A theatre has 18 rows. Row 1 has 26 seats and each later row has 2 more seats than the row before it. Calculate the total number of seats in the theatre.","points":1,"difficulty":"easy","topic":"Arithmetic series","config":{"source":"myp5-sequences-series-e-assessment","question_number":44,"part":"-","chapter":"Arithmetic series","criterion":"D","printed_level":"Level 2","printed_question_marks":1,"marks_printed_in_pdf":"question total only","numeric_answer":774.0,"tolerance":0.0},"options":[]},{"position":93,"type":"ordering","prompt":"Q45 (a). Order the steps to find the sum of the first 20 terms of an arithmetic sequence with a_1 = 6 and d = 3.","points":1,"difficulty":"medium","topic":"Arithmetic series","config":{"source":"myp5-sequences-series-e-assessment","question_number":45,"part":"a","chapter":"Arithmetic series","criterion":"C","printed_level":"Level 3","printed_question_marks":2,"marks_printed_in_pdf":"question total only","correct_order":["Identify a_1 = 6, d = 3, n = 20.","Find the last term: u_20 = 6 + 3(20-1) = 63.","Substitute into S_20 = 20/2 (a_1 + u_20) = 10(6 + 63).","Evaluate S_20 = 690."]},"options":[{"label":"Identify a_1 = 6, d = 3, n = 20.","is_correct":true},{"label":"Find the last term: u_20 = 6 + 3(20-1) = 63.","is_correct":true},{"label":"Substitute into S_20 = 20/2 (a_1 + u_20) = 10(6 + 63).","is_correct":true},{"label":"Evaluate S_20 = 690.","is_correct":true}]},{"position":94,"type":"numeric","prompt":"Q45 (b). The sum of the first 20 terms of an arithmetic sequence with a_1 = 6 and d = 3. Find S_20.","points":1,"difficulty":"medium","topic":"Arithmetic series","config":{"source":"myp5-sequences-series-e-assessment","question_number":45,"part":"b","chapter":"Arithmetic series","criterion":"C","printed_level":"Level 3","printed_question_marks":2,"marks_printed_in_pdf":"question total only","numeric_answer":690.0,"tolerance":0.0},"options":[]},{"position":95,"type":"open_ended","prompt":"Q46 (a). A student finds the sum of the first 12 terms of an arithmetic sequence with a_1 = 5 and d = 3 as S_12 = 12/2 [2(5) + 12(3)] = 276. Identify the error.","points":1,"difficulty":"medium","topic":"Arithmetic series","config":{"source":"myp5-sequences-series-e-assessment","question_number":46,"part":"a","chapter":"Arithmetic series","criterion":"C","printed_level":"Level 4","printed_question_marks":2,"marks_printed_in_pdf":"question total only","marking_criteria":"1 mark. The student used 12d instead of 11d: (n-1)d = (12-1)(3) = 33."},"options":[]},{"position":96,"type":"numeric","prompt":"Q46 (b). For the arithmetic sequence with a_1 = 5 and d = 3, find the correct sum of the first 12 terms.","points":1,"difficulty":"medium","topic":"Arithmetic series","config":{"source":"myp5-sequences-series-e-assessment","question_number":46,"part":"b","chapter":"Arithmetic series","criterion":"C","printed_level":"Level 4","printed_question_marks":2,"marks_printed_in_pdf":"question total only","numeric_answer":258.0,"tolerance":0.0},"options":[]},{"position":97,"type":"open_ended","prompt":"Q47. Explain why S_n = n/2 (a_1 + u_n) and S_n = n/2 [2a_1 + (n-1)d] give the same sum for an arithmetic series. Use the meaning of the average of the first and last terms.","points":2,"difficulty":"hard","topic":"Arithmetic series","config":{"source":"myp5-sequences-series-e-assessment","question_number":47,"part":"-","chapter":"Arithmetic series","criterion":"C","printed_level":"Level 5","printed_question_marks":2,"marks_printed_in_pdf":"question total only","marking_criteria":"2 marks. 1: the average of the first and last terms times n gives the sum. 1: substituting u_n = a_1 + (n-1)d into n/2 (a_1 + u_n) gives n/2 [2a_1 + (n-1)d]."},"options":[]},{"position":98,"type":"numeric","prompt":"Q48 (a). A 10-member tontine: member 1 pays 2,000 XOF, member 2 pays 2,500 XOF, and each later member pays 500 XOF more. Find member 10's contribution (XOF).","points":0.5,"difficulty":"hard","topic":"Arithmetic series","config":{"source":"myp5-sequences-series-e-assessment","question_number":48,"part":"a","chapter":"Arithmetic series","criterion":"D","printed_level":"Level 5","printed_question_marks":2,"marks_printed_in_pdf":"question total only","numeric_answer":6500.0,"tolerance":0.0},"options":[]},{"position":99,"type":"numeric","prompt":"Q48 (b). A 10-member tontine: member 1 pays 2,000 XOF and each later member pays 500 XOF more than the one before. Find the total collected (XOF).","points":1,"difficulty":"hard","topic":"Arithmetic series","config":{"source":"myp5-sequences-series-e-assessment","question_number":48,"part":"b","chapter":"Arithmetic series","criterion":"D","printed_level":"Level 5","printed_question_marks":2,"marks_printed_in_pdf":"question total only","numeric_answer":42500.0,"tolerance":0.0},"options":[]},{"position":100,"type":"open_ended","prompt":"Q48 (c). Explain why using 10d instead of 9d in part (a) would be wrong.","points":0.5,"difficulty":"hard","topic":"Arithmetic series","config":{"source":"myp5-sequences-series-e-assessment","question_number":48,"part":"c","chapter":"Arithmetic series","criterion":"D","printed_level":"Level 5","printed_question_marks":2,"marks_printed_in_pdf":"question total only","marking_criteria":"0.5 mark. Member 10 is 9 steps after member 1, so the common difference is added 9 times (a_10 = a_1 + 9d), not 10 times."},"options":[]},{"position":101,"type":"matching","prompt":"Q49. Match each arithmetic-series description to the correct sum expression. Do not evaluate the sums. Use each choice once.","points":2,"difficulty":"hard","topic":"Arithmetic series","config":{"source":"myp5-sequences-series-e-assessment","question_number":49,"part":"-","chapter":"Arithmetic series","criterion":"A","printed_level":"Level 6","printed_question_marks":2,"marks_printed_in_pdf":"question total only","match_mode":"match","pairs":[{"left":"12 terms with a_1 = 4 and u_12 = 37","right":"$S_{12} = \\tfrac{12}{2}(4 + 37)$"},{"left":"The first 20 positive odd numbers","right":"$S_{20} = \\tfrac{20}{2}[2(1) + 19(2)]$"},{"left":"15 terms with a_1 = 100 and d = -4","right":"$S_{15} = \\tfrac{15}{2}[200 + 14(-4)]$"},{"left":"The sum of u_3 to u_10 for u_n = 5n + 2","right":"$S = \\tfrac{8}{2}(17 + 52)$"}]},"options":[]},{"position":102,"type":"numeric","prompt":"Q50 (a). A farming cooperative delivers 35 seed bags on day 1 and 4 more bags each later day. Find the number of bags delivered on day 14.","points":1,"difficulty":"hard","topic":"Arithmetic series","config":{"source":"myp5-sequences-series-e-assessment","question_number":50,"part":"a","chapter":"Arithmetic series","criterion":"D","printed_level":"Level 6","printed_question_marks":6,"marks_printed_in_pdf":"question total only","numeric_answer":87.0,"tolerance":0.0},"options":[]},{"position":103,"type":"numeric","prompt":"Q50 (b). A farming cooperative delivers 35 seed bags on day 1 and 4 more bags each later day. Find the total number of bags delivered over the first 14 days.","points":2,"difficulty":"hard","topic":"Arithmetic series","config":{"source":"myp5-sequences-series-e-assessment","question_number":50,"part":"b","chapter":"Arithmetic series","criterion":"D","printed_level":"Level 6","printed_question_marks":6,"marks_printed_in_pdf":"question total only","numeric_answer":854.0,"tolerance":0.0},"options":[]},{"position":104,"type":"numeric","prompt":"Q50 (c). A farming cooperative delivers 35 seed bags on day 1 and 4 more bags each later day. Find the minimum number of days needed to deliver at least 1,200 bags in total.","points":2,"difficulty":"hard","topic":"Arithmetic series","config":{"source":"myp5-sequences-series-e-assessment","question_number":50,"part":"c","chapter":"Arithmetic series","criterion":"D","printed_level":"Level 6","printed_question_marks":6,"marks_printed_in_pdf":"question total only","numeric_answer":18.0,"tolerance":0.0},"options":[]},{"position":105,"type":"open_ended","prompt":"Q50 (d). Interpret the result from part (c) for planning.","points":1,"difficulty":"hard","topic":"Arithmetic series","config":{"source":"myp5-sequences-series-e-assessment","question_number":50,"part":"d","chapter":"Arithmetic series","criterion":"D","printed_level":"Level 6","printed_question_marks":6,"marks_printed_in_pdf":"question total only","marking_criteria":"1 mark. The cooperative needs 18 days to deliver at least 1,200 bags (17 days gives 1,139 and 18 days gives 1,242). Planning should allow 18 days."},"options":[]}]$zj$::jsonb;
  v_item jsonb;
  v_qid uuid;
  v_n int;
  v_o int;
  v_qq int;
  v_points numeric;
begin
  if exists (select 1 from public.exams where organization_id = v_org and title = $zq$MYP5 Examination$zq$) then
    raise notice 'MYP5 Examination already exists, nothing to do.';
    return;
  end if;

  insert into public.quizzes (organization_id, title, description, status, settings)
  values (
    v_org,
    $zq$MYP5 Examination$zq$,
    $zq$MYP 5 Sequences & Series E-Assessment. Fifty printed questions (105 scored answer parts), 100 marks, 90 minutes: recursive and explicit formulas, linear sequences, arithmetic sequences, quadratic sequences, geometric sequences and arithmetic series.$zq$,
    'published',
    $zj${"randomize_questions":false,"randomize_options":false}$zj$::jsonb
  )
  returning id into v_quiz;

  for v_item in select value from jsonb_array_elements(v_items) as t(value) order by (value->>'position')::int loop
    insert into public.questions
      (organization_id, type, prompt, prompt_doc, explanation, points, difficulty, topic, tags, status, review_status, config)
    values (
      v_org,
      v_item->>'type',
      v_item->>'prompt',
      null,
      null,
      (v_item->>'points')::numeric,
      v_item->>'difficulty',
      v_item->>'topic',
      array['myp5','sequences-series']::text[],
      'published',
      'approved',
      v_item->'config'
    )
    returning id into v_qid;

    if v_item->>'type' = 'ordering' then
      insert into public.question_options (question_id, label, is_correct, position)
      select v_qid, o->>'label', (o->>'is_correct')::boolean, ord::int
        from jsonb_array_elements(v_item->'options') with ordinality as x(o, ord);
    end if;

    insert into public.quiz_questions (quiz_id, question_id, position, points)
    values (v_quiz, v_qid, (v_item->>'position')::int, (v_item->>'points')::numeric);
  end loop;

  insert into public.exams
    (organization_id, quiz_id, class_id, title, description, instructions, duration_minutes,
     status, access_code, shared_link_enabled, settings)
  values (
    v_org,
    v_quiz,
    null,
    $zq$MYP5 Examination$zq$,
    $zq$MYP 5 Sequences & Series E-Assessment. Fifty printed questions (105 scored answer parts), 100 marks, 90 minutes: recursive and explicit formulas, linear sequences, arithmetic sequences, quadratic sequences, geometric sequences and arithmetic series.$zq$,
    $zq$Answer every part. Show your working where it is asked for. Give units and follow the rounding instruction in each question. A calculator is allowed. Sort and match questions: drag each item onto its target (on a touch screen, tap an item and then tap its target). Ordering questions: drag the steps into order, or use the arrows. Written parts are marked against the criteria shown to the examiner.$zq$,
    90,
    'draft',
    'MYP5-SEQ100-2026',
    false,
    $zj${"randomize_questions":false,"randomize_options":false,"total_marks":100}$zj$::jsonb
  )
  returning id into v_exam;

  select count(*) into v_n from public.questions
   where organization_id = v_org and config ->> 'source' = 'myp5-sequences-series-e-assessment';
  select count(*) into v_o from public.question_options o
    join public.questions q on q.id = o.question_id
   where q.organization_id = v_org and q.config ->> 'source' = 'myp5-sequences-series-e-assessment';
  select count(*), coalesce(sum(points), 0) into v_qq, v_points from public.quiz_questions where quiz_id = v_quiz;

  if v_n <> 105 or v_o <> 12 or v_qq <> 105 or v_points <> 100 then
    raise exception 'MYP5 import incomplete: questions=%, options=%, quiz_questions=%, marks=%', v_n, v_o, v_qq, v_points;
  end if;
end $body$;
